Database migrations for the Session Pro backend.

Any file in this directory beginning with a number and ending in .sql or .py is a one-time migration.
Migrations are processed in ascii-sorted order against the `migrations_applied` ledger: if a
migration's filename is not recorded there, it is executed and its filename is then inserted into
`migrations_applied`. Each migration runs inside its own transaction, so it need not start one
itself; if it fails, the transaction (and the ledger insert) is rolled back.

Name migrations `NNN_description.sql` (e.g. `000_payments.sql`). A migration that depends on an
earlier one must use a larger `NNN`; migrations that depend on nothing else start at 000.

Two kinds of migration are permitted:

- SQL migrations are a file of statements to execute. Structural migrations should be written
  idempotently (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION`, `DROP … IF EXISTS` before
  `CREATE`) so that re-applying the baseline against a database that predates the ledger is a no-op.

- Python migrations are for data/semantic steps that need real logic. Such a file must define a
  function `apply_<FILENAME>` in the session::core::schema sense — i.e. `apply_` followed by the
  filename without its `.py` extension, with every non-alphanumeric character replaced by `_`
  (so `010_backfill_ids.py` → `apply_010_backfill_ids`). It is called with the open connection
  `(conn)`; a transaction is already active around the call, so raising rolls the migration back.

It is not permitted to use the same base filename for both a .sql and a .py migration (e.g. both
`007_foo.sql` and `007_foo.py`): give them different numeric prefixes or suffixes instead.
